--TEST--
Every spelling of a `Core\Task::all` field that is not a written `fn` literal is refused by name — a local, a parameter, a first-class callable and one read out of an array — while the literal beside them compiles — ADR 0072 § *Verification*, ADR 0031
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `Core\Task::all`'s result is a shape carrying each field's own return type,
// and `callable` carries no signature (ADR 0031), so a field holding one has
// no result type to read. ADR 0072 names this as a compile error rather than
// a `mixed` fallback: a silently widened field would make the whole shape's
// types depend on how the caller happened to spell a closure.
//
// This is the agreement shape (`docs/agent/conventions.md`): four different
// ways of *not* writing a literal, asked one question each, so a checker that
// grew a special case for one of them fails here while still looking right on
// the others.
class Held {
    public static function job(): int {
        return 1;
    }

    // A parameter is the spelling that looks most like it should work, because
    // the caller did write a literal — one frame up, where this site cannot
    // see it.
    public static function fromParameter(callable $held): void {
        var $a = Core\Task::all({param: $held});
        echo $a->param;
    }
}

// The other side of the bound, first: a written literal is accepted and its
// field is typed `int`, which is the whole reason the four below cannot be.
var $ok = Core\Task::all({literal: fn(): int => 1});
echo $ok->literal;

callable $held = fn(): int => 1;
var $b = Core\Task::all({local: $held});
echo $b->local;

// A first-class callable has a signature the *reader* can see and the type
// system still does not: `Ty::CallableShapeTo`'s doc owns why the binding
// happens at the literal or not at all.
var $c = Core\Task::all({firstClass: Held::job(...)});
echo $c->firstClass;

array<callable> $bag = [fn(): int => 1];
var $d = Core\Task::all({fromArray: $bag[0]});
echo $d->fromArray;
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0774]: the field `param` must be written as an `fn` literal
%A
error[E0774]: the field `local` must be written as an `fn` literal
%A
error[E0774]: the field `firstClass` must be written as an `fn` literal
%A
error[E0774]: the field `fromArray` must be written as an `fn` literal
%A
error: aborting due to 4 errors
